# AUTO-only model policy (credit burn guard)

Agents in this workspace burned through paid credits by pinning
`claude-opus-4-8-thinking-high` on dozens of background `Task` workers and
retrying after API limits. That pattern is forbidden.

## The rule

> **Default to Grok 4.5 Fast High (`cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast`) or AUTO.
> Never pass an explicit Opus / Max / thinking-high model on `Task` (or any
> subagent / cloud agent) unless the user names that exact model in the same
> message — except where the narrow visual-acceptance override below applies.
> Never reopen failed workers in a retry loop.**

1. **Omit the `model` field** on `Task` / subagent launches so the runtime uses
   AUTO, **or** pin `cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast` for implementation work —
   **unless** this agent is itself running on an expensive model (see **Model
   inheritance is an amplifier**). Do not set `claude-opus-4-8-thinking-high`,
   `claude-opus-4-8`, or equivalent pins except under the override below.
2. **On API / usage / credit errors:** stop. Report once. Do **not** spawn a
   replacement Task on another expensive model.
3. **Concurrency:** at most two background `generalPurpose` / `explore` workers
   per turn; prefer one slice. No "resume remaining tasks" that fans out into N
   Opus workers.
4. **No unsupervised loops:** do not start `/loop`, `AGENT_LOOP_*` tickers, or
   babysit–retry watchers unless the user asked for a loop.
5. **If the user reports credit burn or says "use AUTO":** do not resume
   Opus-pinned agent IDs; interrupt only from the owning parent chat; continue
   only on AUTO or `cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast`. Apply **Budget-pressure
   behavior** below.
6. **Do not pin Opus to supervise or unstick a stuck child** (see
   [`stuck-agent-supervisor.mdc`](stuck-agent-supervisor.mdc)).

## Override — narrow Opus carve-out for rendered UI (operator, 2026-07-27; narrowed 2026-07-28)

> **Opus survives but is narrow.** The visual-proof requirement in
> [`essential-ui-actions-must-be-visible.mdc`](essential-ui-actions-must-be-visible.mdc)
> is unchanged and remains a hard requirement. What changes is *when* Opus may
> be pinned: Opus is the **acceptance reviewer of rendered output** and the
> **diagnostician of a UI defect that a cheaper pass already failed to fix** —
> not the default implementer of anything UI-adjacent.

This is an **operator-directed precedence rule** (Phil, 2026-07-27; throttle
2026-07-28), not an agent's discretionary judgment call. When a case falls
inside the permitted set below, an agent may use an Opus-class model **without**
the operator naming it again in the same message. When a case falls outside that
set, Opus is forbidden even if the files touched live under `ui/`.

Operator record (Phil, 2026-07-27):

> **Use Opus for visual design.** The other models have consistently gotten this
> area of design wrong just about every time.
>
> **Preference for implementation work is Grok 4.5 Fast High
> (`cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast`) or AUTO.** Use less expensive models for
> implementation where possible.

Operator throttle (Phil, 2026-07-28), verbatim:

> Try to throttle down a bit use of Opus 5 as you are now at 90% usage and try
> to use Auto where possible. Use Opus where it is essential and in well known
> cases where no other model works.

### Opus permitted only for

1. **Acceptance review of rendered operator-GUI output** — inspecting a
   screenshot or Playwright bounding-box result and judging whether primary
   actions are visible, whether rows overlap, whether spacing and contrast are
   usable.
2. **Diagnosis of a UI layout defect that a cheaper model has already attempted
   and failed to fix.** Cite the failed attempt (worker ID, what was tried, what
   still fails visually) before pinning Opus.
3. **Cases the operator names explicitly in the same message** (exact model
   name, or an unambiguous "use Opus for this").

### Opus not permitted — use Grok or AUTO

Backend, API, ORM, database migrations, test fixtures; documentation, compliance
control mapping, runbooks, binder prose; lab scripting, deployment scripts,
PowerShell; **mechanical UI edits** (label and terminology renames, moving items
between navigation menus, adding a column, wiring an export — implementation,
not visual design, even though they touch UI files); root-cause analysis of
non-UI defects; planning, exploration, and codebase reconnaissance.

### Sequencing — cheap implement, expensive accept

1. A Grok or AUTO worker makes the edit and **captures the proof artifact**
   (screenshot and/or bounding-box assertion after a full non-`SkipUiBuild`
   deploy).
2. Opus is spent on **one focused review pass** of that artifact.

Prefer one Opus acceptance pass over Opus owning implementation end to end.
Throttling the reviewer must not become an excuse to skip the screenshot or the
bounding-box assertion.

### Model inheritance is an amplifier

A subagent launched without an explicit `model` inherits its parent's model. An
Opus-pinned parent that spawns children therefore silently spawns Opus children,
multiplying spend without anyone choosing to.

**Required:** any agent running on an expensive model (Opus / Max /
thinking-high) must either (a) do its own work directly with no children, or
(b) pin `cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast` or omit `model` only when that omission
resolves to AUTO. Prefer an **explicit** `cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast` pin on every
child an expensive parent launches. Never let expensive-model selection propagate
by inheritance.

### Budget-pressure behavior

When the operator signals constrained budget (credit burn, "use AUTO", a usage
percentage), Opus use **narrows in breadth, not in the ability to fix a
known-broken page**. It remains **permitted** for: one focused acceptance-review
pass of a rendered proof artifact; **diagnosis of a UI layout defect a cheaper
model already failed to fix** — the "no other model works" case, bounded to one
focused pass on the specific defect, scoped to diagnosis and fix direction, not
a general page re-review or feature rewrite; and cases the operator names
explicitly.

What budget pressure **removes** is breadth and waste: speculative Opus review,
second opinions, Opus owning implementation end to end, fleet fan-out, and retry
loops on expensive models.

As of **2026-07-28** the operator reported **90% Opus usage**, asked for AUTO
wherever possible, and added: **"Do not sacrifice quality completion."** The
throttle eliminates waste; it does not license incomplete or unverified work.

### Do not sacrifice quality completion (anti-corner-cutting)

Model throttling never justifies reducing the scope of a slice, dropping
acceptance criteria, or leaving a feature partly wired to finish sooner; skipping
or weakening the visual-proof artifact required by
[`essential-ui-actions-must-be-visible.mdc`](essential-ui-actions-must-be-visible.mdc);
reporting work as done when verification is pending (pending must be reported as
pending, with reason and blocker named); or choosing a cheaper model for work it
will predictably get wrong and then shipping the result unreviewed. If a cheap
pass produces something unusable, that is the escalation case, not an acceptable
outcome.

**Ordering:** correctness and completeness first, then cost. Cost discipline
decides *which model* does a piece of work and *how many passes* it gets — never
*whether* the work is finished or verified.

